<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<worksheet xml:id="Worksheet" >
    <title>Math 1320 Section 1.1</title>

    <subsection >
        <title>Idea 1 – Linear Equations</title>
        <p>
            Linear equations are also known as equations of first degree. That is, variables have <em>no exponents</em>.
        </p>
        <p>
            In General, this would exclude equations with variables in the denominators of fractions, since they would have an 
            exponent of <m>-1</m>. However, we will see some equations with variables in their denominators that are <em>equivalent to</em> 
            linear equations.
        </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection >
        <title>Idea 2 – Solving Equations</title>
        <p>
            To solve an equation means to find all possible solutions.
        </p>
        <p>
            Good News! When solving an equation you can do anything you want—as long as you do it twice. Once to the whole left side and 
            once to the whole right side.
        </p>
        <p>
            The most common things we do:
            <ul>
                <li>Add/subtract same value from both sides</li>
                <li>Multiply/divide both sides by the same non-zero value</li>
            </ul>
        </p>
        <p>
            We solve equations by:
            <ol>
                <li>Distribute to eliminate parentheses</li>
                <li>Combine "Like Terms"</li>
                <li>Use Addition/Subtraction to "collect" variables on one side, constants on the other</li>
                <li>Use Multiplication/Division to isolate the variable</li>
            </ol>
        </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection >
        <title>Solving Linear Equations Video</title>
        
        <p>Watch the video below for a step-by-step explanation:</p>
        
        
            <caption>Solving Linear Equations</caption>
            <interactive iframe="https://echo360.org/media/9d4f201e-b274-47f8-909f-24c4eb74d050/public" />
        
    </subsection>

    <subsection >
        <title>Example</title>
        
        <example>
            <statement>
                <p>Solve:</p>
                <me>x - 4(x - 5) = 2 - (2 - x)</me>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>Step 1: Distribute on both sides</p>
                <me>x - 4x + 20 = 2 - 2 + x</me>
                <me>-3x + 20 = x</me>
                
                <p>Step 2: Collect variables on one side</p>
                <me>-3x - x = -20</me>
                <me>-4x = -20</me>
                
                <p>Step 3: Isolate the variable</p>
                <me>x = 5</me>
                
                <p>Therefore, the solution is <m>x = 5</m>.</p>
            </solution>
        </example>
    </subsection>

    <subsection >
        <title>Clearing Fractions</title>
        
        <figure >
            <caption>Clearing the Fraction</caption>
            <interactive iframe="https://echo360.org/media/ad586bdc-3b3d-4da4-8d1e-832db422fccc/public" />
        </figure>
        
        <p> 
            To clear fractions from an equation:
            <ol>
                <li>Find the Lowest Common Denominator (LCD) of ALL the fractions. (If you can't find the lowest common denominator, just multiply all denominators together.)</li>
                <li>Multiply every term (on BOTH sides) by the LCD</li>
                <li>Simplify each term. There should be no fractions.</li>
                <li>Solve the new equation using your algebra skills.</li>
            </ol>
        </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection >
        <title>Example</title>
        
        <example>
            <statement>
                <p>Solve:</p>
                <me>\frac{2x}{3} - \frac{5x}{6} - 3 = \frac{x}{2} - 5</me>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>LCD = <m>6</m></p>
                
                <p>Step 1: Multiply every term by 6</p>
                <me>6\left(\frac{2x}{3}\right) - 6\left(\frac{5x}{6}\right) - 6(3) = 6\left(\frac{x}{2}\right) - 6(5)</me>
                
                <p>Step 2: Simplify</p>
                <me>4x - 5x - 18 = 3x - 30</me>
                <me>-x - 18 = 3x - 30</me>
                
                <p>Step 3: Solve</p>
                <me>-x - 3x = -30 + 18</me>
                <me>-4x = -12</me>
                <me>x = 3</me>
                
                <p>Therefore, the solution is <m>x = 3</m>.</p>
            </solution>
        </example>
    </subsection>

    <subsection >
        <title>What happens if there is a variable in the denominator?</title>

        <!-- Example 1 - Variables in denominator -->
        <example >
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Solve: 
                    <me>\frac{9}{4} - \frac{1}{2x} = \frac{4}{x}</me>
                </p>
                <p>
                    LCD: <fillin /> 
                    Restricted Values for <m>x</m>: <fillin />
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>LCD = <m>4x</m></p>
                <p>Restricted Values: <m>x \neq 0</m></p>
                
                <p>Step 1: Multiply every term by <m>4x</m></p>
                <me>4x\left(\frac{9}{4}\right) - 4x\left(\frac{1}{2x}\right) = 4x\left(\frac{4}{x}\right)</me>
                
                <p>Step 2: Simplify</p>
                <me>9x - 2 = 16</me>
                
                <p>Step 3: Solve</p>
                <me>9x = 18</me>
                <me>x = 2</me>
                
                <p>Since <m>2 \neq 0</m>, this is a valid solution.</p>
                
                <p>Therefore, the solution is <m>x = 2</m>.</p>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <!-- Example 2 - Variables in denominator -->
        <example >
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Solve: 
                    <me>\frac{1}{x-1} + \frac{x+1}{x^2 + 2x - 3} = \frac{1}{x+3}</me>
                </p>
                <p>
                    LCD: <fillin /> 
                    Restricted Values for <m>x</m>: <fillin />
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>First, factor <m>x^2 + 2x - 3 = (x-1)(x+3)</m></p>
                <p>LCD = <m>(x-1)(x+3)</m></p>
                <p>Restricted Values: <m>x \neq 1</m> and <m>x \neq -3</m></p>
                
                <p>Step 1: Multiply every term by <m>(x-1)(x+3)</m></p>
                <me>(x-1)(x+3)\left(\frac{1}{x-1}\right) + (x-1)(x+3)\left(\frac{x+1}{(x-1)(x+3)}\right) = (x-1)(x+3)\left(\frac{1}{x+3}\right)</me>
                
                <p>Step 2: Simplify</p>
                <me>(x+3) + (x+1) = (x-1)</me>
                <me>x + 3 + x + 1 = x - 1</me>
                <me>2x + 4 = x - 1</me>
                <me>2x - x = -1 - 4</me>
                <me>x = -5</me>
                
                <p>Since <m>-5 \neq 1</m> and <m>-5 \neq -3</m>, this is a valid solution.</p>
                
                <p>Therefore, the solution is <m>x = -5</m>.</p>
            </solution>
        </example>
    </subsection>

